你可以在写太多软件之前，先拼凑出一个好用的样本展示给潜在客户看。
you can put together a useful sample and show it to a potential customer before writing much software.

153
00:14:24,096 --> 00:14:26,084
这就是我喜欢这类业务的原因。
And that's what I like about those types of businesses.

154
00:14:26,574 --> 00:14:33,828
对于那些已经认识很多本地服务提供商的人来说，家庭维修那个创意可能更值得深入研究。
For someone who already knows a bunch of local service providers, the home repair idea might be a closer look.

155
00:14:34,126 --> 00:14:40,576
不过没错，我试着给了你们几种不同类型的创业创意，有的偏向消费者，有的偏向商业。
But yeah, I tried to give you a few different types of startup ideas, more consumer, more business-y.

156
00:14:40,976 --> 00:14:45,536
我猜消费者类的创意做起来最有趣。
My guess is the consumer ideas are the most fun to do.

157
00:14:45,776 --> 00:14:50,116
而且因为这是 Meta，他们更像是一家消费类应用公司，所以这类用例更多。
And because it's meta, they're more of a consumer app company, there's more of those use cases.

158
00:14:50,816 --> 00:14:56,256
但我认为也会有很多钱，而且也会有很多商业用例。
But I think there's going to be a lot of money and also business use cases as well.

159
00:14:56,656 --> 00:15:03,556
当然，这引出了我如果正在观看或收听这段内容时会提出的问题，这很好。
Of course, this brings us to the question I'd have if I was watching or listening to this, which is great.

160
00:15:03,936 --> 00:15:05,668
我有一个连接器。
I have a connector.

161
00:15:05,936 --> 00:15:08,736
人们要怎么知道我的连接器？
How are people going to learn about my connector?

162
00:15:08,816 --> 00:15:10,724
他们要怎么知道我的业务？
How are they going to learn about my business?

163
00:15:11,854 --> 00:15:16,964
显然，进入目录可以获得大量的曝光。
Obviously, getting into the directory gets a lot of exposure.

164
00:15:18,318 --> 00:15:27,108
但一个陌生的服务是否会在一般的对话中被推荐，这仍需要证据支持。
but whether an unknown service is going to get recommended, during a general conversation is something we still need evidence for.

165
00:15:27,470 --> 00:15:32,292
所以我会围绕我能够触达的客户来制定初步计划。
So I'd build my initial plan around customers I have a way to reach.

166
00:15:33,230 --> 00:15:38,212
所以我给你的第一个想法是，与拥有受众的现有创作者合作。
So the first idea I have for you is work with existing creators who have audiences.

167
00:15:38,606 --> 00:15:44,324
比如对于家庭晚餐服务，那可以是一个为家长分享素食菜谱的创作者。
So for the family dinner service, that could be a creator who shares vegetarian recipes for parents.

168
00:15:44,846 --> 00:15:49,956
然后你帮助他们演示如何将几顿饭变成一份购物订单。
And then you help them demonstrate how to turn a few meals into a grocery order.

169
00:15:50,286 --> 00:15:58,244
他们向受众提供经过测试的设置说明，然后你们商定他们为介绍的客户获得多少报酬。
They give their audience the tested setup instructions, and you agree on how they'll be paid for customers they introduce.

170
00:15:58,702 --> 00:16:04,228
观众看到了他们想要完成的任务，并理解了为什么要连接这个服务。
The viewer has seen a task they want to accomplish and understands why they connect the service.

171
00:16:04,590 --> 00:16:10,756
我从一个相关的创作者开始，看看有多少人完成了那个第一个任务。
I start with one relevant creator, and I see how many people complete that first task.

172
00:16:11,118 --> 00:16:17,540
说实话，对我来说，受众的大小没那么重要，重要的是这些人是否需要你卖的东西。
The size of the audience matters less to me, honestly, than whether the people in it need what you're selling.

173
00:16:17,838 --> 00:16:30,176
我给你的第二个想法是关于板球服务，例如，一个人预订
球场，并分享一个页面给另外三名球员，显示时间和地点。
The second idea I have for you is for the paddle service, for example, one person
books a court and shares a page with other three players showing the time and location.

174
00:16:30,336 --> 00:16:34,564
所以这个创意基本上就是产品驱动的病毒式分享。
So this idea is basically product-driven viral sharing.

175
00:16:34,990 --> 00:16:36,932
有些人也称之为产品驱动增长。
Some people call it product-led growth too.

176
00:16:37,518 --> 00:16:41,764
但基本上，就是你如何让一个人去分享你的产品？
But basically, it's how do you get one person to share your product?

177
00:16:42,336 --> 00:16:48,576
在板球服务这个创意中，那些球员在注册之前就能从你的产品中获得有用的东西。
in the paddle service idea, those players get something useful from your product before they ever sign up.

178
00:16:48,942 --> 00:16:55,204
所以如果你给他们一个简单的安排下次比赛的方法，你就有了通往下一次预订的潜在路径。
So if you give them an easy way to arrange the next game, you know, you have a possible route to another booking.

179
00:16:55,694 --> 00:16:59,216
所以你可以选择那些在工作流程中内置了这种分享机制的创意。
So you can choose ideas with this kind of sharing built into the work.

180
00:16:59,296 --> 00:17:06,656
你可以根据这些多人参与的创意来确定优先级，因为这会帮助你推动大量的增长。
You can kind of prioritize based on these multiplayer ideas because that's going to help you, you know, drive a lot of growth.

181
00:17:06,736 --> 00:17:14,736
而且我们看到，在移动互联网时代，很多社交应用，很多应用都加入了社交功能。
And we saw that, you know, in the mobile era, you know, a lot of social apps, a lot of apps had you know social features to it.

182
00:17:14,958 --> 00:17:17,028
而正是这些应用变得非常流行。
And those are the ones that became really popular.

183
00:17:17,964 --> 00:17:21,136
在现在的智能体时代也是如此。
Same is true uh in this agentic era.

184
00:17:21,839 --> 00:17:23,680
那么，举个例子呢？
So, you know, what's an example of that?

185
00:17:23,854 --> 00:17:28,960
比如一份提案发给某人，或者一份活动邀请被传阅。
You know, a proposal gets sent to someone, an event invitation gets passed around.

186
00:17:29,358 --> 00:17:32,804
你要让接收结果的人觉得它有用。
You know, make the result useful to the person receiving it.

187
00:17:33,070 --> 00:17:36,960
然后其中一些人可能会变成客户。
And then some of those people may become customers themselves.

188
00:17:37,040 --> 00:17:37,636
这就是我们的期望。
That's the hope.

189
00:17:38,080 --> 00:17:46,628
所以在称之为增长循环之前，我会先衡量这一点，但当你要在两个或三个有前景的创意中做选择
时，这是一个很实用的考量因素。
So I'd measure that before I'd call it a growth loop, but it's a practical thing
to look for when you're choosing between, you know, two or three promising ideas.

190
00:17:47,118 --> 00:17:52,708
我给你的第三个想法是通过现有的连接市场进行分发。
The third idea I have for you is distribution through existing connected marketplaces.

191
00:17:53,166 --> 00:18:04,132
比如 Ticketmaster 表示，符合条件的活动现在可以通过它的连接器出现，
而不需要每个组织者去做额外的集成工作。
So Ticketmaster says eligible events can now appear through its connector without each organizer doing additional integration work.

192
00:18:04,462 --> 00:18:13,028
这意味着组织者现在可以在继续经营自己的活动业务的同时，
通过 Ticketmaster 的体验接触到用户。
So an organizer now could reach someone through the Ticketmaster experience while continuing to run their own event business.

193
00:18:13,390 --> 00:18:18,660
这让我很好奇每个连接服务是从哪里获取供应的。
That makes me curious about where each connected service gets its supply.

194
00:18:19,040 --> 00:18:32,400
如果你所在的行业已经有了现成的市场，就去调查企业是如何参与其中的，
以及哪些信息能帮助客户准确地选择他们。
If you're in an industry with an existing marketplace, investigate how businesses
participate and what information helps customers choose them accurately.

195
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:36,880
所以对某些人来说，这可能是进入这个市场最务实的方法。
So for some people, that could be the most practical way into this market.

196
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:44,196
对其他人来说，建立自己的连接器更有意义，因为
他们已经有了客户，或者有合作伙伴可以介绍他们。
for others, building their own connector makes sense because they already have customers or a partner who can introduce them.

197
00:18:44,686 --> 00:18:46,560
所以我会尽早做出这个决定。
So I'd make that decision early.

198
00:18:46,926 --> 00:18:58,724
即使你希望目录能成为重要的需求来源，你也得能指明某个特定的人或渠道来为你带来首批客户。
You want to be able to point to a specific person or channel that can bring your first customers,
even while you're hoping the directory becomes a meaningful source of demand.

199
00:18:59,758 --> 00:19:05,828
当然，Route Four 只是在 Meta 的目录里获得了功能展示位。
Of course, Route Four is just getting feature placement, in meta's directory.

200
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:09,440
但咱们说实话，你不能指望仅仅靠那个。
But let's be real, you're not gonna be able to bank on that.

201
00:19:09,920 --> 00:19:16,800
当我拥有那个应用商店功能，也就是第一个应用商店功能时，它基本上有点随机。
when I had that app store feature, that first app store feature, it was just like basically somewhat random.

202
00:19:16,960 --> 00:19:22,400
我醒来突然就想，为什么，为什么图表是垂直的？
I woke up and all of a sudden I was like, why is why is the chart vertically?

203
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:26,084
我觉得很多人在 Meta 目录上的体验也会是这样的。
And I think a lot of people are gonna have that experience with the meta directory.

204
00:19:27,086 --> 00:19:34,052
你有点在赌门洛帕克的某个产品营销经理会觉得「这是一个好应用」。
you're kind of banking on some product marketing manager in Menlo Park to be like, this is a good app.

205
00:19:34,640 --> 00:19:37,764
所以你对构建连接器感兴趣。
So you're interested in building a connector.

206
00:19:38,240 --> 00:19:42,148
我该如何着手构建第一个版本呢？
How would I approach building the first version?

207
00:19:42,880 --> 00:19:51,332
对我来说，我实际上会从写下一件客户应该能够完成的事情开始，听起来虽然很简单。
And for me, I would actually start with writing down one thing the customer should be able to accomplish, as basic as that sounds.

208
00:19:51,790 --> 00:19:57,380
比如对于球场预约的创意，我会从「给我看看明天晚上我附近的可用球场」开始。
So for the paddle idea, I'd start with show me the available courts near me tomorrow evening.

209
00:19:57,710 --> 00:20:00,164
然后还要包含每个球场的价格。
And then there's a price, for each one.

210
00:20:00,590 --> 00:20:09,444
你可以把这个简述交给像 Claude Code 或 Codex 这样的编程智能体，
然后把你要连接的系统的文档给它就行了。
So you can take that brief to a coding agent like Claude Code or Codex, and then
you just give it the documentation for the system that you're connecting to.

211
00:20:09,838 --> 00:20:17,520
然后我会用最普通的英语或者语言来描述客户的需求。
And then I would describe the customer request in just like plain ordinary English or language.

212
00:20:17,902 --> 00:20:25,540
如果你在构建一个新服务，在弄清楚流程的同时，先从带有清晰标签的样本库存开始。
If you're building a new service, start with just clearly labeled sample inventory while you're figuring out the flow.

213
00:20:25,998 --> 00:20:35,360
我会要求它先构建可用性检查和报价，结果需要显示完整价格以及价格的有效期。
I'd ask it to build the availability check and the quote first, and the
result needs to show the full price and how long the price is valid.

214
00:20:35,630 --> 00:20:40,164
当然，一旦这些部分运行正常，就可以进行预订操作了。
